## Build and run

Requires `gcc` and `make` on Linux, macOS, or WSL.

```sh
make            # build ./tscos
./tscos         # scripted demo: five scenarios from the report (Chapter 11)
./tscos -i      # interactive mode: type commands and watch the signals respond
make test       # run the automated checks
```

## Scripted demo

`./tscos` runs the five scenarios from the report: adaptive rush-hour control,
emergency vehicle preemption, pedestrian crossing, round-robin fallback, and manual
police override. Its output matches `docs/execution_log.txt`.

## Interactive mode

```
tscos> help
  density N S E W     set vehicle counts for the four approaches
  rush 0|1            turn rush-hour profile off/on
  emergency N|S|E|W   emergency vehicle on an approach (preempts)
  ped N|S|E|W         pedestrian button pressed at a crossing
  override N|S|E|W    police force one approach green
  release             release police override (safe transition)
  help                show this list
  quit                stop the system
```

Example session:

```
tscos> density 45 12 8 15     → adaptive: North green for 50 s
tscos> emergency E            → ambulance on East: East green for 25 s
tscos> ped W                  → all vehicles red, West pedestrians WALK 15 s
tscos> density 5 5 4 5        → equal traffic: round-robin N → E → S → W
tscos> override S             → police lock South green
tscos> release                → yellow, then red, automatic mode resumes
```

A full transcript is in `docs/interactive_example.txt`.

## Dynamic green time

In rush-hour mode the green time for the busiest approach is

    green = 20 + (2 × density) / 3      (capped at 60 s)

Normal mode uses 30 s. Densities count as equal when the highest and lowest counts
differ by at most one vehicle, which triggers round-robin.

## Limitations

- This is a **simulation**. Sensors and signal lamps are simulated; there is no
  hardware driver (the report's Chapter 9 describes the planned Raspberry Pi / ESP32
  design).
- Time is a **virtual clock**, so a multi-minute sequence runs instantly while the
  printed timestamps stay realistic.
- Emergency and pedestrian requests are handled in a single pass; the full
  22-step loop from the report is represented by `scheduler_step()`.

